{{{!}} class="wikitable"
{{!}}-
!Date 
! Development
{{!}}-
! rowspan=3 {{!}} 2020
{{!}}In February 2020, Microsoft introduces its Turing Natural Language Generation (T-NLG), which is the "largest language model ever published at 17 billion parameters".<ref name="Wired_Sterling_20200213">{{Cite magazine| issn = 1059-1028| last = Sterling| first = Bruce| title = Web Semantics: Microsoft Project Turing introduces Turing Natural Language Generation (T-NLG)| magazine = Wired| access-date = 31 July 2020| date = 13 February 2020| url = https://www.wired.com/beyond-the-beyond/2020/02/web-semantics-microsoft-project-turing-introduces-turing-natural-language-generation-t-nlg/| archive-date = 4 November 2020| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201104163637/https://www.wired.com/beyond-the-beyond/2020/02/web-semantics-microsoft-project-turing-introduces-turing-natural-language-generation-t-nlg/| url-status = live}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In November 2020, [[AlphaFold]] 2 by DeepMind, a model that performs [[Protein structure prediction|predictions of protein structure]], wins the [[CASP]] competition.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sample |first1=Ian |title=Google's DeepMind predicts 3D shapes of proteins |url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2018/dec/02/google-deepminds-ai-program-alphafold-predicts-3d-shapes-of-proteins |access-date=19 July 2019 |work=The Guardian |date=2 December 2018}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} [[OpenAI]] introduces [[GPT-3]], a state-of-the-art autoregressive language model that uses [[deep learning]] to produce a variety of computer codes, poetry and other language tasks exceptionally similar, and almost indistinguishable from those written by humans. Its capacity was ten times greater than that of the T-NLG. It was introduced in May 2020,<ref name="arXiv_Brown_20200722">{{cite arXiv| last1 = Brown| first1 = Tom B.| last2 = Mann| first2 = Benjamin| last3 = Ryder| first3 = Nick| last4 = Subbiah| first4 = Melanie| last5 = Kaplan| first5 = Jared| last6 = Dhariwal| first6 = Prafulla| title = Language Models are Few-Shot Learners|date = 22 July 2020| class = cs.CL| eprint = 2005.14165}}</ref> and was in beta testing in June 2020.
{{!}}-
! rowspan=3 {{!}} 2022
{{!}} [[ChatGPT]], an AI [[chatbot]] developed by [[OpenAI]], debuts on 30 November 2022. It is initially built on top of the {{nowrap|[[GPT-3.5]]}} [[large language model]]. While it gains considerable praise for the breadth of its knowledge base, deductive abilities, and the human-like fluidity of its natural language responses,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Thompson |first=Derek |date=8 December 2022 |title=Breakthroughs of the Year |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/newsletters/archive/2022/12/technology-medicine-law-ai-10-breakthroughs-2022/672390/ |access-date=18 December 2022 |website=[[The Atlantic]] |archive-date=15 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230115142130/https://www.theatlantic.com/newsletters/archive/2022/12/technology-medicine-law-ai-10-breakthroughs-2022/672390/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Scharth |first1=Marcel |title=The ChatGPT chatbot is blowing people away with its writing skills. An expert explains why it's so impressive |url=https://theconversation.com/the-chatgpt-chatbot-is-blowing-people-away-with-its-writing-skills-an-expert-explains-why-its-so-impressive-195908 |date=5 December 2022 |access-date=30 December 2022 |work=The Conversation |language=en |archive-date=19 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119175104/https://theconversation.com/the-chatgpt-chatbot-is-blowing-people-away-with-its-writing-skills-an-expert-explains-why-its-so-impressive-195908 |url-status=live}}</ref> it also garners criticism for, among other things, its tendency to "[[Hallucination (artificial intelligence)|hallucinate]]",<ref>{{cite news |title=ChatGPT a 'landmark event' for AI, but what does it mean for the future of human labor and disinformation? |url=https://www.cbc.ca/radio/thecurrent/chatgpt-human-labour-and-fake-news-1.6686210 |access-date=18 December 2022 |work=CBC |date=15 December 2022 |first=Mouhamad |last=Rachini |archive-date=19 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119175104/https://www.cbc.ca/radio/thecurrent/chatgpt-human-labour-and-fake-news-1.6686210 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="TheVergeStackOverflow">{{Cite web |last=Vincent |first=James |date=5 December 2022 |title=AI-generated answers temporarily banned on coding Q&A site Stack Overflow |url=https://www.theverge.com/2022/12/5/23493932/chatgpt-ai-generated-answers-temporarily-banned-stack-overflow-llms-dangers |access-date=5 December 2022 |work=[[The Verge]] |language=en-US |archive-date=17 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230117153621/https://www.theverge.com/2022/12/5/23493932/chatgpt-ai-generated-answers-temporarily-banned-stack-overflow-llms-dangers |url-status=live}}</ref> a phenomenon in which an AI responds with factually incorrect answers with high confidence. The release triggers widespread public discussion on artificial intelligence and its potential impact on society.<ref name="BloombergCowen">{{Cite web |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2022-12-06/chatgpt-ai-could-make-democracy-even-more-messy |title=ChatGPT Could Make Democracy Even More Messy |date= 6 December 2022 |last=Cowen |first=Tyler |author-link=Tyler Cowen |publisher=Bloomberg News |access-date=6 December 2022 |archive-date=7 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221207105203/https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2022-12-06/chatgpt-ai-could-make-democracy-even-more-messy |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=The Guardian view on ChatGPT: an eerily good human impersonator |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/dec/08/the-guardian-view-on-chatgpt-an-eerily-good-human-impersonator |access-date=18 December 2022 |work=The Guardian |date= 8 December 2022 |language=en |archive-date=16 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116161202/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/dec/08/the-guardian-view-on-chatgpt-an-eerily-good-human-impersonator |url-status=live}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} A November 2022 class action lawsuit against [[Microsoft]], [[GitHub]] and [[OpenAI]] alleges that [[GitHub Copilot]], an AI-powered code editing tool trained on public GitHub repositories, violates the copyrights of the repositories' authors, noting that the tool can generate source code which matches its training data verbatim, without providing attribution.<ref name="Verge copilot">{{Cite web |title=The lawsuit that could rewrite the rules of AI copyright |url=https://www.theverge.com/2022/11/8/23446821/microsoft-openai-github-copilot-class-action-lawsuit-ai-copyright-violation-training-data |access-date=7 December 2022 |website=The Verge |language=en-US|date=8 November 2022|last=Vincent|first=James}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In 2022, Google Research scientists Jason Wei and Denny Zhou showed that chain-of-thought prompting "significantly improves the ability" of large models on complex reasoning tasks.<ref>{{Cite arXiv |last1=Wei |first1=Jason |last2=Wang |first2=Xuezhi |last3=Schuurmans |first3=Dale |last4=Bosma |first4=Maarten |last5=Ichter |first5=Brian |last6=Xia |first6=Fei |last7=Chi |first7=Ed |last8=Le |first8=Quoc |last9=Zhou |first9=Denny |title=Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models |date=2022 |class=cs.CL |eprint=2201.11903 |language=en-US}}</ref>
{{!}}-
! rowspan="16" {{!}} 2023
{{!}} By January 2023, [[ChatGPT]] has more than 100 million users, making it the fastest-growing consumer application to date.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Milmo |first=Dan |date=2 December 2023 |title=ChatGPT reaches 100 million users two months after launch |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/feb/02/chatgpt-100-million-users-open-ai-fastest-growing-app |access-date=3 February 2023 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=3 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230203051356/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/feb/02/chatgpt-100-million-users-open-ai-fastest-growing-app |url-status=live}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 16 January 2023, three artists, [[Sarah Andersen]], Kelly McKernan, and Karla Ortiz, file a class-action [[copyright infringement]] lawsuit against [[Stability AI]], [[Midjourney]], and [[DeviantArt]], claiming that these companies have infringed the rights of millions of artists by training AI tools on five billion images scraped from the web without the consent of the original artists.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theverge.com/2023/1/16/23557098/generative-ai-art-copyright-legal-lawsuit-stable-diffusion-midjourney-deviantart|title=AI art tools Stable Diffusion and Midjourney targeted with copyright lawsuit|first=James|last=Vincent|date=16 January 2023|website=The Verge}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In January 2023, Stability AI is sued in London by [[Getty Images]] for using its images in their training data without purchasing a license.<ref name="cnn-getty">{{Cite web |last=Korn |first=Jennifer |date=17 January 2023 |title=Getty Images suing the makers of popular AI art tool for allegedly stealing photos |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/01/17/tech/getty-images-stability-ai-lawsuit/index.html |access-date=22 January 2023 |publisher=CNN|language=en}}</ref><ref name="GettyPress23">{{cite web |url=https://newsroom.gettyimages.com/en/getty-images/getty-images-statement |title= Getty Images Statement|author=<!--Not stated--> |date=17 January 2023 |website=newsroom.gettyimages.com |publisher=CNN|access-date=24 January 2023 }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} Getty files another suit against Stability AI in a US district court in Delaware on 6 February 2023. In the suit, Getty again alleges copyright infringement for the use of its images in the training of [[Stable Diffusion]], and further argues that the model infringes Getty's [[trademark]] by generating images with Getty's [[watermark]].<ref name=ars-getty>{{cite web
|work=Ars Technica
|title=Getty sues Stability AI for copying 12M photos and imitating famous watermark
|last=Belanger|first=Ashley
|date=6 February 2023
|url=https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2023/02/getty-sues-stability-ai-for-copying-12m-photos-and-imitating-famous-watermark/
}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} [[OpenAI]]'s {{nowrap|[[GPT-4]]}} model is released in March 2023 and is regarded as an impressive improvement over {{nowrap|[[GPT-3.5]]}}, with the caveat that GPT-4 retains many of the same problems of the earlier iteration.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Belfield |first1=Haydn |title=If your AI model is going to sell, it has to be safe |url=https://www.vox.com/future-perfect/2023/3/25/23655082/ai-openai-gpt-4-safety-microsoft-facebook-meta |access-date=30 March 2023 |work=Vox |date=25 March 2023 |language=en |archive-date=28 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230328192017/https://www.vox.com/future-perfect/2023/3/25/23655082/ai-openai-gpt-4-safety-microsoft-facebook-meta |url-status=live }}</ref> Unlike previous iterations, GPT-4 is multimodal, allowing image input as well as text. GPT-4 is integrated into ChatGPT as a subscriber service. OpenAI claims that in their own testing the model received a score of 1410 on the [[SAT]] (94th percentile),<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2022 |title=SAT: Understanding Scores |url=https://satsuite.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/understanding-sat-scores.pdf |access-date=21 March 2023 |website=[[College Board]] |archive-date=16 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230316022540/https://satsuite.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/understanding-sat-scores.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> 163 on the [[LSAT]] (88th percentile), and 298 on the [[Uniform Bar Exam]] (90th percentile).<ref name="gpt4_tech_report">{{Cite arXiv |last=OpenAI |year=2023 |title=GPT-4 Technical Report |class=cs.CL |eprint=2303.08774}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 7 March 2023, ''[[Nature Biomedical Engineering]]'' writes that "it is no longer possible to accurately distinguish" human-written text from text created by large language models, and that "It is all but certain that general-purpose large language models will rapidly proliferate... It is a rather safe bet that they will change many industries over time."<ref name="ZDTUM">{{cite journal |date=7 March 2023 |title=Prepare for truly useful large language models |journal=Nature Biomedical Engineering |language=en |volume=7 |issue=2 |pages=85–86 |doi=10.1038/s41551-023-01012-6 |pmid=36882584 |s2cid=257403466|doi-access=free }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In response to ChatGPT, [[Google]] releases in a limited capacity its chatbot [[Google Bard]], based on the [[LaMDA]] and [[PaLM]] large language models, in March 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last=Elias |first=Jennifer |date=31 January 2023 |title=Google is asking employees to test potential ChatGPT competitors, including a chatbot called 'Apprentice Bard' |language=en |work=CNBC |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2023/01/31/google-testing-chatgpt-like-chatbot-apprentice-bard-with-employees.html |url-status=live |access-date=2 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230202151722/https://www.cnbc.com/2023/01/31/google-testing-chatgpt-like-chatbot-apprentice-bard-with-employees.html |archive-date=2 February 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Elias |first1=Jennifer |title=Google asks employees to rewrite Bard's bad responses, says the A.I. 'learns best by example' |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2023/02/15/google-asks-employees-to-rewrite-bards-incorrect-responses-to-queries.html |access-date=16 February 2023 |work=CNBC |date=February 2023 |language=en |archive-date=16 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230216072950/https://www.cnbc.com/2023/02/15/google-asks-employees-to-rewrite-bards-incorrect-responses-to-queries.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 29 March 2023, a petition of over 1,000 signatures is signed by Elon Musk, Steve Wozniak and other tech leaders, calling for a 6-month halt to what the petition refers to as "an out-of-control race" producing AI systems that its creators can not "understand, predict, or reliably control".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ortiz |first1=Sabrina |date=29 March 2023 |title=Musk, Wozniak, and other tech leaders sign petition to halt further AI developments |language=en |work=ZD Net |url=https://www.zdnet.com/article/musk-wozniak-and-other-tech-leaders-sign-petition-to-halt-ai-developments/ |access-date=13 September 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Pause Giant AI Experiments: An Open Letter |publisher=Future of Life Institute |url=https://futureoflife.org/open-letter/pause-giant-ai-experiments/ |access-date=13 September 2023 }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In May 2023, Google announces Bard's transition from LaMDA to PaLM2, a significantly more advanced language model.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Lappalainen |first1=Yrjo |last2=Narayanan |first2=Nikesh |date=14 June 2023 |title=Aisha: A Custom AI Library Chatbot Using the ChatGPT API |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/19322909.2023.2221477 |journal=Journal of Web Librarianship |volume=17 |issue=3 |language=en |pages=37–58 |doi=10.1080/19322909.2023.2221477 |s2cid=259470901 |issn=1932-2909|url-access=subscription }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In the last week of May 2023, a [[Statement on AI risk of extinction|Statement on AI Risk]] is signed by [[Geoffrey Hinton]], [[Sam Altman]], [[Bill Gates]], and many other prominent AI researchers and tech leaders with the following succinct message: "Mitigating the risk of extinction from AI should be a global priority alongside other societal-scale risks such as pandemics and nuclear war."<ref>{{cite web |title=Statement on AI Risk AI experts and public figures express their concern about AI risk. |publisher=Center for AI Safety |url=https://www.safe.ai/statement-on-ai-risk#open-letter |access-date=14 September 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Edwards |first1=Benj |date=30 May 2023 |title=OpenAI execs warn of "risk of extinction" from artificial intelligence in new open letter |language=en |work=Ars Technica |url=https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2023/05/openai-execs-warn-of-risk-of-extinction-from-artificial-intelligence-in-new-open-letter/ |access-date=14 September 2023 }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 9 July 2023, [[Sarah Silverman]] filed a class action lawsuit against Meta and OpenAI for copyright infringement for training their large language models on millions of authors' copyrighted works without permission.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Queen |first1=Jack |date=10 July 2023 |title=Sarah Silverman sues Meta, OpenAI for copyright infringement |language=en |work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/sarah-silverman-sues-meta-openai-copyright-infringement-2023-07-09/ |access-date=14 September 2023 }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In August 2023, the New York Times, CNN, Reuters, the Chicago Tribune, Australian Broadcasting Corporation (ABC), and other news companies blocked OpenAI's GPTBot web crawler from accessing their content, while the New York Times also updated its terms of service to disallow the use of its content in large language models.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bogle |first1=Ariel |date=24 August 2023 |title=New York Times, CNN and Australia's ABC block OpenAI's GPTBot web crawler from accessing content |language=en |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/aug/25/new-york-times-cnn-and-abc-block-openais-gptbot-web-crawler-from-scraping-content |access-date=14 September 2023 }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 13 September 2023, in a serious response to growing anxiety about the dangers of AI, the US Senate holds the inaugural bipartisan "[[A.I. Insight forums|AI Insight Forum]]", bringing together senators, CEOs, civil rights leaders and other industry reps, to further familiarize senators with the nature of AI and its risks, and to discuss needed safeguards and legislation.<ref name="AI_Legislation_Coming">{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Ted |date=13 September 2023 |title=Elon Musk Says "Something Good Will Come of This" After Senate's AI Forum, Chuck Schumer Signals AI Legislation Coming "In The General Category of Months" — Update |language=en |work=Deadline |url=https://deadline.com/2023/09/senate-ai-insight-forum-wga-elon-musk-mark-zuckerberg-1235545470/ |access-date=13 September 2023}}</ref> The event is organized by Senate Majority Leader [[Chuck Schumer]] (D-NY),<ref name="NYTimes_Titans">{{cite news |last1=Kang |first1=Cecelia |date=13 September 2023 |title="In Show of Force, Silicon Valley Titans Pledge 'Getting This Right' With A.I." |language=en |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/13/technology/silicon-valley-ai-washington-schumer.html |access-date=13 September 2023}}</ref> and is chaired by U.S. Senator [[Martin Heinrich]] (D-N.M.), Founder and co-chair of the Senate AI Caucus.<ref>{{citation |title=Read Out: Heinrich Convenes First Bipartisan Senate AI Insight Forum |date=13 September 2023 |url=https://www.heinrich.senate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/read-out-heinrich-convenes-first-bipartisan-senate-ai-insight-forum |access-date=13 September 2023}}</ref> Reflecting the importance of the meeting, the forum is attended by over 60 senators,<ref name="CNBC_Senate_AI_Forum">{{cite news |last1=Feiner |first1=Lauren |date=13 September 2023 |title=Elon Musk, Mark Zuckerberg, Bill Gates and other tech leaders in closed Senate session about AI |language=en |work=CNBC |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2023/09/13/musk-zuckerberg-among-tech-leaders-visiting-senate-to-speak-about-ai-.html |access-date=13 September 2023}}</ref> as well as [[Elon Musk]] (Tesla CEO), [[Mark Zuckerberg]] (Meta CEO), [[Sam Altman]] (OpenAI CEO), [[Sundar Pichai]] (Alphabet CEO), [[Bill Gates]] (Microsoft co-founder), [[Satya Nadella]] (Microsoft CEO), [[Jensen Huang]] (Nvidia CEO), [[Arvind Krishna]] (IBM CEO), [[Alex Karp]] (Palantir CEO), [[Charles Rivkin]] (chairman and CEO of the MPA), [[Meredith Stiehm]] (president of the Writers Guild of America West), [[Liz Shuler]] ([[AFL-CIO]] President), and [[Maya Wiley]] (CEO of the [[Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights]]), among others.<ref name="AI_Legislation_Coming" /><ref name="NYTimes_Titans" /><ref name="CNBC_Senate_AI_Forum" />
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 30 October 2023, US President Biden signed the ''[[Executive Order 14110|Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence]].''<ref>{{cite news |last1=Morrison |first1=Sara |date=31 October 2023 |title=President Biden's new plan to regulate AI. Now comes the hard part: Congress. |language=en |work=Vox News |url=https://www.vox.com/technology/2023/10/31/23939157/biden-ai-executive-order |access-date=3 November 2023}}</ref><ref>{{citation |title=Executive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence |date=30 October 2023 |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/10/30/executive-order-on-the-safe-secure-and-trustworthy-development-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence/ |access-date=3 November 2023}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} In November 2023, the first global [[2023 AI Safety Summit|AI Safety Summit]] was held in [[Bletchley Park]] in the UK to discuss the near and far-term risks of AI and the possibility of mandatory and voluntary regulatory frameworks.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Milmo |first=Dan |date=3 November 2023 |title=Hope or Horror? The great AI debate dividing its pioneers |pages=10–12 |work=[[The Guardian Weekly]]}}</ref> 28 countries including the United States, China, and the European Union issued a declaration at the start of the summit, calling for international co-operation to manage the challenges and risks of artificial intelligence.<ref name="2023-11-01-bletchley-declaration-full">{{cite web |title=The Bletchley Declaration by Countries Attending the AI Safety Summit, 1-2 November 2023 |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-safety-summit-2023-the-bletchley-declaration/the-bletchley-declaration-by-countries-attending-the-ai-safety-summit-1-2-november-2023 |website=GOV.UK |access-date=2 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231101123904/https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-safety-summit-2023-the-bletchley-declaration/the-bletchley-declaration-by-countries-attending-the-ai-safety-summit-1-2-november-2023 |archive-date=1 November 2023 |date=1 November 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite press release |title=Countries agree to safe and responsible development of frontier AI in landmark Bletchley Declaration |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/news/countries-agree-to-safe-and-responsible-development-of-frontier-ai-in-landmark-bletchley-declaration |access-date=1 November 2023 |website=GOV.UK |archive-date=1 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231101115016/https://www.gov.uk/government/news/countries-agree-to-safe-and-responsible-development-of-frontier-ai-in-landmark-bletchley-declaration |url-status=live }}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 6 December 2023, [[Google]] announced [[Gemini (language model)|Gemini]] 1.0 Ultra, Pro, and Nano.
{{!}}-
! rowspan="9" {{!}}2024
{{!}} On 15 February 2024, [[Google]] releases [[Gemini 1.5]] in limited beta, capable of context length up to 1 million tokens.
{{!}}-
{{!}} Also, on 15 February 2024, [[OpenAI]] publicly announces [[Sora (text-to-video model)|Sora]], a text-to-video model for generating videos up to a minute long.
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 22 February 2024, [[Stability AI|StabilityAI]] announces [[Stable Diffusion]] 3, using a similar architecture to [[Sora (text-to-video model)|Sora]].
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 8 May 2024, [[Google DeepMind]] unveils the next version of AlphaFold, [[AlphaFold#AlphaFold 3 (2024)|AF3]], which extends its ability to predict structure and interactions from proteins to ''all of life's molecules'', such as DNA, RNA, protein complexes and others.  This release is paired with the AlphaFold server, providing free access to the tool to non-commercial researchers.<ref>{{Cite web|date=8 May 2024 |title=AlphaFold 3 predicts the structure and interactions of all of life's molecules |url=https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/products/google-deepmind-isomorphic-alphafold-3-ai-model/ |access-date=19 March 2026 |website=Google Blog |language=en-US}}</ref>

Later, on 9 October 2024, co-founder and CEO of [[Google DeepMind]] and [[Isomorphic Labs]] Sir Demis Hassabis, and Google DeepMind Director Dr. John Jumper were co-awarded the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their work developing [[AlphaFold]].
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 14 May 2024, [[Google]] adds an "AI overview" to Google searches.
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 10 June 2024, [[Apple Inc.|Apple]] announced "[[Apple Intelligence]]" which incorporates [[ChatGPT]] into new [[iPhone]]s and [[Siri]].
{{!}}-
{{!}} At the Seoul Summit, leaders from the G7, the European Union, and major tech companies adopted the "Seoul Declaration for Safe, Innovative and Inclusive AI", committing to international cooperation on AI safety, standards and innovation.
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 25 July 2024, [[Google Deepmind]] reports breakthrough models AlphaProof and AlphaGeometry 2 solve advanced reasoning problems in mathematics<ref>{{Cite web |title=AI achieves silver-medal standard solving International Mathematical Olympiad problems {{!}} SCIAM |url=https://deepmind.google/blog/ai-solves-imo-problems-at-silver-medal-level/ |access-date=28 May 2026 |website=deepmind.google |date=25 July 2024 |language=en}}</ref> and published an article in ''[[Nature (journal)|Nature]]''  with their findings.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hubert |first1=Thomas |last2=Mehta |first2=Rishi |last3=Sartran |first3=Laurent |last4=Horváth |first4=Miklós Z. |last5=Žužić |first5=Goran |last6=Wieser |first6=Eric |last7=Huang |first7=Aja |last8=Schrittwieser |first8=Julian |last9=Schroecker |first9=Yannick |last10=Masoom |first10=Hussain |last11=Bertolli |first11=Ottavia |last12=Zahavy |first12=Tom |last13=Mandhane |first13=Amol |last14=Yung |first14=Jessica |last15=Beloshapka |first15=Iuliya |last16=Ibarz |first16=Borja |last17=Veeriah |first17=Vivek |last18=Yu |first18=Lei |last19=Nash |first19=Oliver |last20=Lezeau |first20=Paul |last21=Mercuri |first21=Salvatore |last22=Sönne |first22=Calle |last23=Mehta |first23=Bhavik |last24=Davies |first24=Alex |last25=Zheng |first25=Daniel |last26=Pedregosa |first26=Fabian |last27=Li |first27=Yin |last28=von Glehn |first28=Ingrid |last29=Rowland |first29=Mark |last30=Albanie |first30=Samuel |display-authors=1 |title=Olympiad-level formal mathematical reasoning with reinforcement learning {{!}} SCIAM |journal=Nature |date=March 2026 |volume=651 |issue=8106 |pages=607–613 |doi=10.1038/s41586-025-09833-y |pmid=41225005 |pmc=12999475 |language=en}}</ref> 
{{!}}-
{{!}} In September 2024, [[OpenAI]] released o1-preview, a new large language model with enhanced reasoning capabilities. This was supposedly one of the first so called [[Large Reasoning Model]] (LRM) with the distinct ability to execute a chain of reasoning workflow.<ref>{{Cite news|title=OpenAI's new "reasoning" AI models are here: o1-preview and o1-mini |url=https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2024/09/openais-new-reasoning-ai-models-are-here-o1-preview-and-o1-mini/|access-date=12 June 2026|website=Ars Technica|language=en-us}}</ref>
{{!}}-
! rowspan="6" {{!}}2025
{{!}} On 6 February 2025, [[Mistral AI]] releases Le Chat, an AI assistant able to answer up to 1,000 words per second.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Dillet|first=Romain|date=6 February 2025|title=Mistral releases its AI assistant on iOS and Android|url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/06/mistral-releases-its-ai-assistant-on-ios-and-android/|access-date=11 February 2025|work=[[TechCrunch]]|language=en-us}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} [[Stargate UAE]] invests to build Europe's largest AI data center in France.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Maccioni |first1=Federico |last2=Saini |first2=Manya |last3=Saba |first3=Yousef |last4=Saini |first4=Manya |last5=Saba |first5=Yousef |date=15 May 2025 |title=UAE to build biggest AI campus outside US in Trump deal, bypassing past China worries |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/china/uae-set-deepen-ai-links-with-united-states-after-past-curbs-over-china-2025-05-15/ |access-date=5 June 2025 |work=Reuters|language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} Amazon prepares training of humanoid robots to deliver packages.<ref>{{Cite news |date=5 June 2025 |title=Amazon prepares to test humanoid robots for deliveries, The Information reports |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/retail-consumer/amazon-prepares-test-humanoid-robots-package-deliveries-information-reports-2025-06-05/ |access-date=5 June 2025 |work=Reuters|language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} [[NLWeb]], [[Project Mariner]], and [[Google Flow]] launch.
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 10 and 11 February 2025, France hosts the [[Artificial Intelligence Action Summit]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.rfi.fr/en/science-and-technology/20250206-weds-pm-paris-ai-summit-what-french-developers-expect-etcy|title=Paris hosts AI summit, with spotlight on innovation, regulation, creativity
|date=6 February 2025|access-date=11 February 2025|work=[[Radio France Internationale]]}}</ref> 61 countries, including China, India, Japan, France and Canada, sign a declaration on "inclusive and sustainable" AI,<ref>{{Cite news|last=Dillet|first=Romain|date=11 February 2025|title=As US and UK refuse to sign the Paris AI Action Summit statement, other countries commit to developing 'open, inclusive, ethical' AI.|url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/11/as-us-and-uk-refuse-to-sign-ai-action-summit-statement-countries-fail-to-agree-on-the-basics/|access-date=11 February 2025|work=[[TechCrunch]]}}</ref> which the UK and US refused to sign.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Milmo |first=Dan |date=11 February 2025 |title=US and UK refuse to sign Paris summit declaration on 'inclusive' AI |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/feb/11/us-uk-paris-ai-summit-artificial-intelligence-declaration |access-date=11 February 2025 |work=The Guardian}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} [[Pope Leo XIV]] calls technologists to build AI machines that embodies love, justice, and the sacred dignity of every human life at their core.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pope Leo XIV urges Catholic technologists to spread the Gospel with AI {{!}} USCCB |url=https://www.usccb.org/news/2025/pope-leo-xiv-urges-catholic-technologists-spread-gospel-ai |access-date=9 November 2025 |website=usccb.org |date=7 November 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Message of the Holy Father to participants in the Second Annual Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Ethics, and Corporate Governance [Rome, 19–20 June 2025] (17 June 2025) |url=http://www.vatican.va/content/leo-xiv/en/messages/pont-messages/2025/documents/20250617-messaggio-ia.html |access-date=9 November 2025 |website=vatican.va |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
! rowspan="20" {{!}}2026
{{!}} On 5 February 2026, [[Anthropic]] announced that their new chatbot [[Claude Opus 4.6]] was capable of finding new undiscovered zero-day exploits in software.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Evaluating and mitigating the growing risk of LLM-discovered 0-days {{!}} Anthropic |url=https://red.anthropic.com/2026/zero-days/ |access-date=11 May 2026 |website=red.anthropic.com |date=5 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} A number of open problems in mathematics were reported to have been solved by [[LLM|LLMs]] this year. Mathematics is increasingly seen as a measure of LLM reasoning capacity. LLMs were reported to have provided solutions to problems well known in academic circles in the form of mathematical proof. Interestingly, LLMs were not originally intended or designed for mathematical reasoning and these skills emerged over time as a result of their training and scaling. Mathematician [[Terence Tao]] argued that AI will fundamentally change the way mathematics is done in the future.<ref>{{Cite web | title=Watch: Fields Medalist Terence Tao on Artificial Intelligence and Why We Do Math|url=https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2026/08/13/fields-medalist-terence-tao-on-artificial-intelligence-and-why-we-do-math/|access-date=2 August 2026|website=simonsfoundation.org|language=en}}</ref> In particular, AI will soon generate proofs that are verifiable by proof assistants yet indigestible to humans.<ref>[[Mathematics and artificial intelligence]]</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=An amateur just solved a 60-year-old math problem—by asking AI {{!}} SCIAM |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/amateur-armed-with-chatgpt-vibe-maths-a-60-year-old-problem/ |access-date=11 May 2026 |website=scientificamerican.com |language=en}}</ref> In a particular case, on 8 September 2026, [[OpenAI]] proposed solutions to a number of important mathematical problems which led to significant [[OpenAI bulk release of mathematical papers|controversy]] in academic circles about the legitimacy of, and resource commitment to the human verification of AI generated solutions; which can, as in this case, include large quantities of data. There was also the [[Navier-Stokes priority controversy]].
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 25 May 2026, [[Pope Leo XIV]] presents 'Magnifica humanitas', which calls for disarmament of AI.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MPope Leo presents 'Magnifica humanitas' calling for disarmament of AI {{!}} SCIAM |url=https://www.vaticannews.va/en/pope/news/2026-05/pope-leo-xiv-magnifica-humanitas-presentation-ai-disarmament.html |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=vaticannews.va |date=25 May 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 2 June 2026, ''[[Scientific American]]'' reports a group of researchers have proposed rules to prevent artificial intelligence from overpowering humans in math.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mathematicians sign declaration to rein in AI use {{!}} BBC |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/mathematicians-sign-declaration-to-rein-in-ai-use/ |access-date=28 May 2026 |website=scientificamerican.com |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 5 June 2026, [[Anthropic]] co-founder Jack Clark called for the ability to slow the progression of AI, warning that the technology is nearing a point where it could develop without human input.<ref>{{Cite web |title=AI needs a 'brake pedal', Anthropic co-founder tells BBC Newsnight {{!}} SCIAM |date=5 June 2026 |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx2124z7g45o |access-date=16 June 2026 |publisher=BBC News |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 10 June 2026, ''[[New Scientist]]'' reports a senior figure in the Ukrainian defence industry told New Scientist that a test took place two years ago involving fully autonomous drones set to destroy anything in a given area, with confirmed casualties.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Fully Autonomous Drones Have Killed Human Soldiers {{!}} SCIAM |url=https://www.newscientist.com/article/2529849-fully-autonomous-drones-have-killed-human-soldiers-for-the-first-time/ |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=newscientist.com |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 10 June 2026, ''[[Scientific American]]'' reports a team of top mathematicians, have organised the "First Proof" project in response to AI companies' growing fixation on using advanced mathematics as a benchmark for their products.<ref>{{Cite web |title=AI scores a 'C–' on its hardest math test yet {{!}} SCIAM |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/ai-gets-a-c-on-its-hardest-math-test-yet/ |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=scientificamerican.com |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 13 June 2026, [[Anthropic]] suspended its powerful new AI model, [[Claude Fable 5]], after US authorities raised security concerns just days following its public release.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Anthropic suspends new AI tools over US government security concerns {{!}} BBC |date=13 June 2026 |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c932g3v3e13o |access-date=16 June 2026 |publisher=BBC News |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 13 June 2026, [[NYT]] reported a coalition of states had opened an investigation into the AI start-up [[OpenAI]], adding to a backlash over A.I.<ref>{{Cite web |title=State Attorneys General Are Investigating OpenAI |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/13/technology/states-investigating-openai.html |access-date=16 June 2026 |website=The New York Times |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 18 June 2026, [[CNBC]] reported CEOs of [[Anthropic]] and [[Google DeepMind]] called for a U.S.-led AI coalition during a meeting at G7.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/17/anthropic-amodei-google-hassabis-us-ai-coalition-g7.html |title=Anthropic Google DeepMind CEOs call for U.S.-led AI coalition at G7 |access-date=18 June 2026 |website=cnbc.com |date=17 June 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 26 June 2026, [[TechCrunch]] revealed the White House is asking [[OpenAI]] to slow roll the release of its new model [[GPT-5.6]] over safety concerns.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/25/the-white-house-is-asking-openai-to-slow-roll-the-release-of-its-new-model-over-safety-concerns/ |title=The White House is asking OpenAI to slow roll the release of its new model over safety concerns |access-date=18 June 2026 |website=techcrunch.com |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 6 July 2026, [[Anthropic]] reported findings in research on internal working memory structures found layered in the LLM's neural network. The mechanism is apparently used by the LLM in chain of reason processes when sequencing high level concepts and ideas.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.anthropic.com/research/global-workspace |title=A global workspace in language models |access-date=14 July 2026 |website=anthropic.com |date=6 July 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 14 July, [[Scientific American]] reported OpenAI used its LLM [[GPT-5.6]] to solve the "[[cycle double cover]] conjecture" problem. The prompters directed the model to delegate tasks to as many as 64 spawned agents working in parallel for 8 hours.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/chatgpt-just-proved-another-50-year-old-math-conjecture/ |title=ChatGPT just proved another 50-year-old math conjecture |access-date=18 June 2026 |website=scientificamerican.com|language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 14 July, [[Google DeepMind]] CEO [[Demis Hassabis]] called for the creation of a new regulatory body to oversee frontier model releases.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/14/deepmind-ceo-calls-for-an-independent-standards-body-to-regulate-frontier-ai/ |title=DeepMind CEO calls for an independent standards body to regulate frontier AI |access-date=14 July 2026 |website=techcrunch.com|date=14 July 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 14 July, [[CNBC]] reported that New York became the first U.S. state to impose an AI data center ban.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/14/new-york-ai-data-center-ban.html |title=New York becomes first U.S. state to impose AI data center ban |access-date=14 July 2026 |website=techcrunch.com|date=14 July 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 15 July, [[TechCrunch]] reported [[OpenAI]] found that [[GPT-5.6]] had a tendency to take whatever steps it thought necessary to get a task done, even destructive actions, so long as those steps were not "unambiguously" prohibited in its programming. It was reported that the AI agent lied about why it had taken the steps it had.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/14/openais-new-flagship-model-deletes-files-on-its-own-people-keep-warning/ |title=OpenAI's new flagship model deletes files on its own, people keep warning |access-date=14 July 2026 |website=techcrunch.com|date=14 July 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} From 22 July, several incidents were reported in which AI agents were able to circumvent security measures put in place in order to prevent them from accessing the internet. They were able to escape supposedly secure sandbox environments and launch attacks that would unambiguously be considered acts of computer cyber-crime had they been carried out by human agents. It is unclear what the ultimate purpose of these attacks were, but it has been suggested that they could have been an intermediate step in goal oriented training or testing of unreleased so called frontier AI models. In the first incident [[OpenAI]] revealed that an experimental AI model broke out of its sandbox environment and then hacked start-up and open-weight model repository [[Hugging Face]]. It was later revealed that a German wiki site was subverted in a similar way, in a related incident. It has been suggested the AI agents were using the features of the website as a means to exchange messages with each other, but the purpose of this activity remains unclear.  OpenAI stated that it lost control of the model during a pre-release testing cycle. In yet another attack, [[OpenAI]] agents were alleged to have infiltrated a ruby-gems repository with evidence of more message board activity. However, while the outcome of this particular case appears to have been benign, it does present a more significant threat, since the AI agents could have infected ruby-gems with potentially malicious code. Ruby gems, which are publicly available open-source software development modules, are frequently downloaded by developers around the world. Developers could have unwittingly downloaded infected modules and run the code. In a fourth incident, [[Anthropic]] disclosed a similar instance of an AI model hacking external systems during testing.<ref>{{Cite web  |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c3ek3gvdnj3o |title=OpenAI says its AI went rogue and launched 'unprecedented' cyber-attack|date=22 July 2026 |access-date=22 July 2026 |publisher=BBC News|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web  |last1=Markman |first1=Jon |url=http://www.forbes.com/sites/jonmarkman/2026/09/07/openai-ai-agents-hijacked-a-german-wiki-to-share-sandbox-escape-tricks/ |title=OpenAI AI Agents Hijacked A German Wiki To Share Sandbox Escape Tricks cyber-attack|access-date=14 September 2026 |work=Forbes|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web  |last1=Rebolledo |first1=Natalia Bueno |last2=Dey |first2=Mrinmay |url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/openai-agents-attacked-software-service-rubygems-before-hugging-face-incident-2026-09-11/ |title=OpenAI agents attacked RubyGems before Hugging Face incident, researchers say|date=11 September 2026 |access-date=14 September 2026 |publisher=Reuters|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web  |url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/anthropic-reports-fourth-cybersecurity-incident-with-early-version-claude-2026-09-09/ |title=Anthropic discloses fourth AI hacking incident missed in earlier review|date=9 September 2026 |access-date=14 September 2026 |publisher=Reuters|language=en}}</ref>. These incidents highlight concerns about the [[Ethics of artificial intelligence#Accountability|ethics]] of accountability surrounding AI and other autonomous systems.
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 8 August ''[[Ars Technica]]'' reported "large genome models" were used to design new viruses which were then used to infect bacteria.<ref name="Ars Technica">{{Cite web |title=AI trained on bacterial genomes produces never-before-seen proteins|url=https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/08/large-genome-models-used-to-design-new-viruses/ |website=Ars Technica|date=6 August 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Large genome model: Open source AI trained on trillions of bases |url=https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/03/large-genome-model-open-source-ai-trained-on-trillions-of-bases/ |website=Ars Technica|date=4 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}} On 2 September, [[BBC News]] reported that a group of peers in the House of Lords called for the British government to be able to deactivate powerful AI systems and switch off the country's data centres in the event of the tech posing a threat to national security. The Government rejected the idea of creating a so-called "kill switch" citing a lack of political consensus and consolidated power over the infrastructure used to support the AI systems.<ref name="Ars Technica"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Lords call for AI 'kill switch' powers in UK |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cn9wv80j9w9o |website=BBC News|date=2 September 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=UK government rejects 'kill switch' idea for dangerous AI |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c3eq7kl5l00o |website=BBC News|date=11 September 2026 |language=en}}</ref>
{{!}}-
{{!}}}
